      },
      "stale_hits": [],
      "global_stale_hits": [],
      "answer": "TL;DR: Thalia was **not corrupted**, she was **ensorcelled (magically manipulated) by the Wolf**, who used that control to direct her actions\u2014meaning she was played, not willingly complicit.\n\nThalia\u2019s state is consistently OBSERVED as **\u201censorcelled (unbeknownst to her)\u201d and \u201censorcelled by the Wolf,\u201d** while also explicitly **\u201cdoes not have an oily sheen to her eyes.\u201d** In contrast, the oily sheen is a clear marker of corruption seen in guards and the Wolf. This strongly separates **corruption (physical/visible influence)** from **ensorcellment (covert magical control)**.\n\nHer behavior supports this: she **attacks the group after the Wolf\u2019s instruction** and **serves the Wolf**, but her mental state includes **\u201cInnocent\u201d** and later **\u201cRealizes how dire the situation truly is\u201d** once the **charmed status ended**. That indicates her agency returns when the influence breaks.\n\nThe Wolf\u2019s role ties directly in: he is a **cultist**, manipulator, and deceiver who **orders Thalia to attack** and works to mislead others. Thalia\u2019s misplaced trust and defensive stance toward the guard align with **being controlled and misled**, not corrupted.\n\nKey Attributes:\n- Thalia physical_condition: ensorcelled; no oily sheen  \n- Thalia mental_state: Innocent; charmed status ended; later realizes truth  \n- Thalia loyalty: serves the Wolf (under influence), protects city  \n- The Wolf role: cultist, manipulator, issues commands to Thalia  \n- Corruption marker: oily sheen (absent in Thalia, present elsewhere)",
      "embedding_similarity": null
    }
